<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="62354" href="schedule/2015/spring/FIN/580/62354.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2015" href="schedule/2015.xml">2015</calendarYear><term id="120151" href="schedule/2015/spring.xml">Spring 2015</term><subject id="FIN" href="schedule/2015/spring/FIN.xml">Finance</subject><course id="580" href="schedule/2015/spring/FIN/580.xml">Special Topics in Finance</course></parents><sectionNumber>BP2</sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Business and Policy Analysis</sectionTitle><sectionDateRange>Meets 16-Mar-15 - 06-May-15.</sectionDateRange><creditHours>2 hours</creditHours><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>Today?s corporate and public policy leaders make decisions in strategic and highly complex environments.  Designing effective programs and policies to solve business and/or policy problems requires a fundamental understanding of the causal impact these programs and policies will have.  This course will develop the skills necessary to distinguish between correlation and causation when using data to assess the effectiveness of programs and policies to achieve target objectives.</sectionText><partOfTerm>B</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>Open (Restricted)</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2015-03-16Z</startDate><endDate>2015-05-06Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>12:30 PM</start><end>01:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>MW     </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>2001</roomNumber><buildingName>Business Instructional Fac</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Molitor" firstName="D">Molitor, D</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>